858:## Tests that another suite already has
859-
860-Benchmarks overlap. CIS, CISA SCuBA and ASD all ask for multifactor authentication for every user, and Maester
861-has its own test for it as well. Before you write a test for a benchmark requirement, look for a test that
862-already reads the same setting, then decide from the benchmark's own sources whether it asks for anything that
863-test does not check.
864-
865-1. **Find the tests that read the setting.** `Get-MtTest -Product entra -Feature conditional-access | Format-Table
866-   Id, Title` lists the candidates; searching `tests/` for the Graph endpoint or the setting name finds the rest.
867-   Look in every suite, not only the one you are writing for.
868-2. **Read what the benchmark asks, in its own sources.** The one-line text of a requirement is usually generic.
869-   The detail that decides between two implementations is in the benchmark's audit or assessment guidance and in
870-   any reference configuration it publishes: the audit and remediation sections of a CIS benchmark, the SCuBA
871-   baseline and ScubaGear for CISA, and for ASD the ISM, the Essential Eight assessment process guide and the ASD
872-   Blueprint for Secure Cloud. Note every concrete value they name: a number of days, a threshold, a list of
873-   roles, an authentication strength, a rule that must be set to Block.
874-3. **Choose what to write:**
875-
876-| What the benchmark asks | What you write |
877-| --- | --- |
878-| What an existing test checks | A shim: a test with its own ID, title, `Control` and guide whose body returns the result of the existing test |
879-| The same check with a different value, which the existing test takes as a parameter | A shim that passes the value, with the benchmark's value as the default of its own parameter |
880-| Something no test checks, or a rule no parameter covers | A test of its own. Read the data with the helpers in `powershell/internal/services/<service>/`, and move a helper there when another suite has the code you need; don't copy another test's logic |
